From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 14:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fingerprint: add an AIDL backend

biomd only speaks the HIDL fingerprint HAL,
android.hardware.biometrics.fingerprint@2.1. From Android 13 a new vendor
image only has to implement the AIDL interface, and on those devices the
HIDL service is not merely unused, it is absent: init cannot start fps_hal
because /vendor/bin/hw/...fingerprint@2.1-service does not exist, while
servicemanager lists
android.hardware.biometrics.fingerprint.IFingerprint/default and the
vendor.fingerprint-default process is up and healthy. biomd then logs
"Failed to get fingerprint remote service" and gives up, and the sensor is
dead on a device whose HAL is working perfectly.

This is not device specific - it is every vendor that has moved, which from
A13 onwards is all of them.

Add a second backend behind the existing FingerprintBackend vtable, which
was already HAL agnostic, so nothing above it changes. fingerprint.c tries
AIDL first and falls back to HIDL; neither constructor is destructive when
its HAL is missing, each just fails to find its service and returns NULL, so
a HIDL-only device is unaffected beyond one extra servicemanager lookup.

The AIDL HAL differs from HIDL in more than names:

 - It lives on /dev/binder with the ordinary servicemanager rather than on
   /dev/hwbinder with hwservicemanager.

 - It is session based. IFingerprint::createSession() hands out an ISession
   for a (sensor, user) pair and everything happens on that. The session is
   opened once at init and kept.

 - There is no global cancel. enroll() and authenticate() each return an
   ICancellationSignal, so cancel goes to whichever signal the running
   operation handed back.

 - Error dropped LOCKOUT, which is now its own onLockoutTimed and
   onLockoutPermanent callbacks, and AcquiredInfo shifted every value by one
   to make room for UNKNOWN. Both are translated back onto the values
   fingerprint.c and the D-Bus API already speak, so the vocabulary above
   the backend is unchanged. onAuthenticationFailed is reported the way HIDL
   reported a rejected touch, as a non-terminal UNABLE_TO_PROCESS, which is
   what the per-touch miss feedback on the lockscreen expects.

 - onEnrollmentsEnumerated and onEnrollmentsRemoved deliver the whole set in
   one array where HIDL called back once per finger with a countdown.
   fingerprint.c finalises on remaining == 0, so the array is replayed as
   that sequence - including one call for an empty array, or the enrolled
   list is never reconciled.

 - The gatekeeper handshake that mints an enrollment token is AIDL too, and
   returns a structured HardwareAuthToken rather than the packed 69-byte
   hw_auth_token_t, so the token goes straight from IGatekeeper::verify into
   ISession::enroll without being flattened. The password handle is kept in
   the same /var/lib/biomd/handle-<uid>.blob the HIDL path uses. A device
   with no AIDL gatekeeper still authenticates and removes; only enroll may
   be refused, and that is logged rather than being fatal.

Transaction codes are FIRST_CALL_TRANSACTION plus the method's index in the
.aidl file, since declaration order is the wire contract; the enums are
transcribed from the interface definitions and must stay in that order.

Note for integrators: libgbinder picks the /dev/binder protocol and
servicemanager flavour from ApiLevel in /etc/gbinder.conf, and the
servicemanager flavour changes at API 35 (aidl5) and 36 (aidl6). A level
that is too low means the AIDL service lookup fails and this backend never
sees the HAL at all, so ApiLevel has to match the vendor's API level.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
